{
  "concept": "A tangent line at P is the limiting position of the secant line through P and a second point Q as Q slides along the curve toward P, and this single limiting definition reproduces the classical tangent to a circle.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "panel (a): x-axis and y-axis with arrowheads and labels x, y",
    "panel (a): smooth open curve (a gentle hump) drawn in cyan",
    "panel (b): a full circle drawn in cyan",
    "fixed point P marked as a filled dot with label 'P' on the curve/circle",
    "movable point Q marked as a filled dot with label 'Q' on the curve/circle",
    "live secant line through P and Q drawn in magenta, labeled 'Secant line'",
    "family of fainter magenta ghost secant lines fanning out from P showing intermediate positions of Q",
    "tangent line at P drawn as the steepest magenta line, labeled 'Tangent line'",
    "shaded magenta wedge between the tangent line and a secant line",
    "curved grey arrow indicating the rotational sweep of the secant toward the tangent",
    "live slope readout annotation showing the current secant slope and the limiting tangent slope",
    "panel captions '(a)' and '(b)'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "qPosition",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Position of Q (toward P)",
      "range": [0, 1, 0.01],
      "default": 0.7,
      "teaches": "As Q slides toward P the secant line visibly rotates and its slope number settles onto a single value — that limiting value is the slope of the tangent line, so the tangent is just the secant 'in the limit'"
    },
    {
      "id": "panel",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Curve type",
      "options": ["Smooth curve (a)", "Circle (b)"],
      "default": "Smooth curve (a)",
      "teaches": "The same secant-to-tangent limiting process works on an arbitrary smooth curve and on a circle, confirming the limit definition agrees with the familiar geometric tangent to a circle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pPosition",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "Dragging P to a new spot on the curve recomputes the whole fan of secants and the tangent there, showing the tangent direction depends on where on the curve you take the limit"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A secant through two points",
      "narration": "With Q far from P, the line through P and Q is an ordinary secant that cuts across the curve; its slope is just the average rate of change between the two points.",
      "state": { "qPosition": 0.85, "panel": "Smooth curve (a)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Q slides toward P",
      "narration": "Drag the slider so Q moves along the curve toward P: the secant line rotates and the slope readout changes continuously, tracing the fan of intermediate secants.",
      "state": { "qPosition": 0.4, "panel": "Smooth curve (a)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The limit is the tangent",
      "narration": "As Q reaches P the secant line coincides with the tangent line and the slope readout stops changing — that limiting slope is the tangent's slope, the core of the derivative.",
      "state": { "qPosition": 0.02, "panel": "Smooth curve (a)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same limit on a circle",
      "narration": "Switch to the circle and repeat: the secant lines again rotate into a single limiting line at P, which is exactly the classical tangent to the circle — the definition generalizes.",
      "state": { "qPosition": 0.4, "panel": "Circle (b)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the base point P",
      "narration": "Drag P to a new location on the curve or circle and watch the entire fan and the tangent line re-aim, showing the tangent direction is a property of the chosen point on the curve.",
      "state": { "qPosition": 0.4, "panel": "Circle (b)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The tangent line is not a separate construction — it is simply the limiting position of secant lines PQ as Q approaches P, one definition that works for any smooth curve and reduces to the usual tangent on a circle.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Lay out two panels side by side. Left panel (a): light background, black x and y axes with arrowheads, a cyan smooth hump-shaped curve rising then falling; place P on the rising left portion and Q to its right. Right panel (b): a cyan circle with P on the upper-left arc and Q on the upper-right arc. From P draw several magenta secant lines of increasing steepness (the fan), the shallowest passing through the current Q and labeled 'Secant line', and the steepest labeled 'Tangent line'. Fill the wedge between the tangent and a mid secant with translucent magenta and add a curved grey arrow sweeping from the secant toward the tangent to indicate the rotational limit. Mark P and Q as solid dots with italic labels. Show a small live text readout of the secant slope that converges to the tangent slope as the slider goes to 0. Keep colors consistent: cyan curve/circle, magenta lines, grey rotation arrow."
}

